# ISSUE-172: the single-run freshness stamp go-toolchain's test producer writes and
# its coverage producer consumes, within one gate invocation. Scratch state by
# construction — a TRACKED stamp would dirty the tree on every gate run.
.backstop/go-coverage-fresh

# CI RUN DETRITUS — files the workflow's own steps leave in the workspace.
#
# The gate's diff scope INCLUDES UNTRACKED FILES, and several steps run before it:
# the engine-tools step downloads ast-grep.zip, and the gate step's own --json-out
# writes gate-report.json (ISSUE-099 collapsed the separate diagnostic capture into
# the blocking run; the report is still produced, by the gate itself). Measured on
# run 30386673582, which reported 11 in-scope files of which TWO were these — CI was
# gating its own build output. cover.out was already ignored, which is why it never
# appeared and is the precedent this follows.
#
# The report is written AFTER this run's scope is already computed, so it cannot
# contaminate its own run — but an un-ignored one would land in the NEXT run's
# untracked set, and TestCIWorkflow_LeavesNoUngitignoredDroppings fails either way.
ast-grep.zip
gate-report.json

# PM operational queue — the mechanism ships, the correspondence does not.
# Escalations/proposals to the founder are session-ops, not SDLC artifacts;
# historical snapshots remain in pre-flip history as accepted fossils.
.backstop/pm/

# ISSUE-122 e2e fixture exception. `node_modules/` above is a real ignore rule, but
# cmd/backstop/testdata/noncorpus-declared/ PLANTS an artifact-shaped file inside a
# node_modules tree on purpose: the fixture proves core excludes that tree because a
# PACK DECLARES the name, not because core knows it. The fixture is worthless if git
# swallows the very directory under test, so this one path is re-included.
!cmd/backstop/testdata/noncorpus-declared/node_modules/
